In article <2sajej$iq6@mother.usf.edu>, gumbo@virtu.sar.usf.edu (Leonhard Gumbo (nc)) writes:
>What are the exact circumstances that affect choking? I ask this after 
>having choked when I had neither been satiated to begin with nor been 
>informed that I was having difficulty getting my food down, something 
>which had never happened to me before. 

The source is a bit complicated here... but it seems to me that you should 
never choke if you were not satiated at the beginning. The 'choke' function 
is called only if a 'canchoke' flag is set (and it is set only if you were 
satiated at the beginning) and your current 'hunger' is above 2000. 
Alternatively you can choke if you are eating a non-food item, and hunger 
is above 2000 (but what non-food item can give you over 1000 nutrion units 
to move you there from non-satiated state?)

You get a 'Stop eating?' warning if your hunger is above 1500 and you have 
a chance to stop (i.e. you are eating something that requires more than one 
turn to eat).
